{
	title: './test stays clean: no lint warnings, tautologies or leftovers',
	description: "./test fails on any oxlint warning, and the tree has none (questions.md item 65). No tautological tests: a test that restates a one-line function, repeats a constant, or checks its own helper is deleted, starting with the list in task sj's notes (item 64); tasks/README.md says so next to the test rules. Tests that spawn ./run or make temporary homes (/tmp/hal-test-home-<pid> and the like) remove them when they finish, so a test run leaves nothing in /tmp (item 67).",
	status: 'done',
	needs: [],
	foldInto: ['sj', 'kn', 'p'],
	notes: [
		'Lint: 17 warnings, 11 of them no-control-regex on escape-byte regexes a terminal program needs. .oxlintrc.json turns that one rule off (with a comment; oxlint reads JSONC) instead of scattering disable comments; ./test runs oxlint --deny-warnings.',
		"Deleted as tautological: terminal 'restart and quit use different exit codes' (repeats a constant) and the four conventions.test.ts tests of the file's own helpers (layer rule, colour-literal finder, lineCount, sizeProblems). Kept from sj's list, because each catches a plausible wrong implementation: parseModelId (empty provider or model, a second slash in openrouter ids), paths.display (a sibling like $HOME-other must not become ~other) and terminal 'init is idempotent' (a second init must not double key delivery).",
		'The leftovers were not /tmp/hal-test-home-<pid> (none appeared) but $TMPDIR/hal-tab.XXXXXX files, over 3,000 on the dev Mac: ./run makes one per run and removes it in an EXIT trap, which SIGKILL (how e2e and startup tests stop ./run) skips. Those tests now set TMPDIR to their temp home. To find leftovers, run `TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d) bun test src` and list that dir; on macOS tmpdir() is /var/folders/..., not /tmp.',
		'startup.test.ts flaked under parallel agents (medians 103-133 ms). It now takes the fastest start rather than the median: other load only adds time, while added startup work slows every start. Joining still sometimes measures ~99 ms on all five starts on an idle M-series Mac, so the 100 ms joining budget has almost no margin; a regression there shows at once.'
	]
}
